This folder contains physics tests: small simulations of the shipped model
whose results are graded against values derived from first principles.

Why this is separate from the fingerprint suite
-----------------------------------------------

A fingerprint says that the simulation followed the same trajectory as when
the fingerprint was recorded. That makes it an excellent change detector and a
poor oracle: it cannot say whether the trajectory was right, and the moment a
legitimate change is accepted and the baseline re-recorded, every claim the
old fingerprint was making is gone.

The checks here are meant to survive that re-record. Each one states what the
model ought to produce and grades the run against it, so a refactoring that
changes a fingerprint can be accepted with evidence rather than with a
judgement call -- and one that breaks the physics fails a named assertion that
says which quantity moved and by how much.

Why this is separate from tests/unit
------------------------------------

The tests in tests/unit exercise classes without a running simulation: each
one's %activity section becomes the body of a cSimpleModule in a one-module
network. A physics test runs a real simulation of a real network, so it has no
C++ of its own at all -- only an ini, and a grader that reads the results. The
two directories share the opp_test machinery and nothing else.

Running the tests
-----------------

    ./runtest                # all tests, against the release library
    ./runtest Foo.test ...   # selected tests
    MODE=debug ./runtest     # against the debug library

or, from the project root,

    make physicstests
    make MODE=debug physicstests

Both the Simu5G and the INET shared library must be built in the requested
mode, and SIMU5G_ROOT and INET_ROOT must be set (source the setenv script of
both projects). Generated files and the results of each run end up in ./work,
which is not under version control.

These tests run real simulations and are correspondingly slower than the unit
tests -- tens of seconds each rather than a fraction of a second, because the
tolerance of a statistical check is set by how many samples the run collects.

Writing a test
--------------

A test is an opp_test case with no %activity: an %inifile section holding one
configuration, an %extraargs line selecting it by name, and a
%postrun-command that grades the results:

    %extraargs: -c HarqResidualLoss
    %postrun-command: ../../physics_reference.py --grade results >verdict.txt
    %contains: verdict.txt
    graded=27 failures=0

A configuration that sweeps iteration variables runs every point in one test,
and the grader grades each. It picks which set of checks to apply from the
configuration name recorded in the result file, so the name in the ini, the
key in physics_reference.py's GRADERS table and the file name have to agree.

The expected value of a check is computed in physics_reference.py from the
scenario's own parameters. It is never read from a previous run, and the
grader is never adjusted to make a test pass: when the model and the grader
disagree, the grader is right until the physics says otherwise. This is the
same discipline the path-loss tests in tests/unit follow with
pathloss_reference.py.

Reading the results
-------------------

physics_reference.py reads .sca files through omnetpp.scave.results -- the
same library the IDE and opp_scavetool use -- and not by parsing the file
format itself. That is worth insisting on: a hand-written parser in a test
script is one more thing to get subtly wrong, and the library already merges
the iteration variables of each run into the result rows, which is exactly
what a swept test needs to key its checks on.

It needs omnetpp/python on PYTHONPATH, which sourcing the OMNeT++ setenv
script does, and pandas.

One thing to know about that API. get_scalars() defaults to
convert_to_base_unit=True, which rescales values by the statistic's declared
unit -- and applies that to the ':count' field as well, although a count has
no unit. A statistic declared in bytes therefore reports a packet count
multiplied by eight. The grader passes convert_to_base_unit=False and takes
the numbers the run recorded. Note also that the filter expression of
get_runattrs() selects runs rather than attribute names, so an attribute is
picked out of the returned frame afterwards rather than in the filter.

Tolerances
----------

The tolerance policy is stated once, in physics_reference.py, rather than per
test. A check over a fraction of N independent trials is graded against a
K-sigma binomial band with K = 4, which puts the false-failure rate of a
correct model at about one in 16000 per check.

The band is only as tight as the sample count allows, so each verdict line
prints its own deviation in sigmas and the half-width that was applied. That
is what tells a reader how discriminating a case actually was: in
HarqResidualLoss the weakest point, perDl = 0.5 with maxHarqRtx = 3, has a
band of +-27% of the expected value, which is still enough to reject an
off-by-one in the number of transmission attempts by seven sigma.

A check that compares two counts of the same events -- rather than a count
against a probability -- is graded against a small absolute tolerance instead,
and the reason for its size is written at the check.

What a test should be able to do
--------------------------------

Fail. A check that cannot fail is not evidence, so when adding one, run the
scenario once with the mechanism under test deliberately broken and confirm
that the verdict says so. HarqResidualLoss rests on the transmission attempts
being independent, which is what harqReduction = 1 buys; run it with the
default harqReduction of 0.2 and 18 of its 27 checks fail, between 15 and 46
sigma out.

Tripwires
---------

As in tests/unit, some checks grade nothing about the model: they assert a
precondition the other checks silently depend on. HarqResidualLoss checks that
the per-attempt error rate is still perDl, because if it is not then its
residual-loss figure is measuring something else -- and a tripwire failing
first, with its own name, is a great deal more informative than the check it
guards failing for no visible reason.
